"""Prepared reference oracle for FlagGems operator ``floor_divide_scalar``.

Used only for the review phase ``--phase reference-as-solution``: the file is
loaded through the project's existing ``--override`` mechanism, so it is the
observed solution, not a candidate implementation. It does not import, wrap or
call ``flag_gems`` or the candidate under review, therefore it cannot re-enter
the replaced export as its own oracle.

Observed callsites in the unchanged frozen suites (marker
``floor_divide_scalar``):

* ``tests/test_floor_divide.py::test_floor_divide_mixed``
  -> ``flag_gems.floor_divide(x, y)``, expected ``torch.div(x, y, rounding_mode="floor")``
* ``tests/test_floor_divide.py::test_floor_divide_scalar_tensor``
  -> ``flag_gems.floor_divide(x, y)``, expected ``torch.div(x, y, rounding_mode="floor")``
* ``tests/test_floor_divide.py::test_floor_divide_scalar_scalar``
  -> ``flag_gems.floor_divide(a, b)`` with Python scalars,
  expected ``torch.floor_divide(a, b)``
* ``benchmark/test_floor_divide.py::test_floor_divide_scalar``
  -> ``op(inp, 3)`` for ``consts.INT_DTYPES`` inputs

Hence the per-phase keys differ (accuracy public export ``floor_divide``,
benchmark ``op_name`` ``floor_divide_scalar``) while the same entrypoint below
serves both, keeping ``--candidate`` and ``--reference-source`` identical.
"""

import torch

__all__ = ["run"]


def run(a, b):
    """Element-wise floor division with native ATen semantics.

    ``torch.floor_divide(a, b)`` is the same ATen operator as
    ``torch.div(a, b, rounding_mode="floor")``; the frozen tests use both
    spellings to build their own expected values, so dtype promotion, output
    dtype, device, shape and rounding mode here follow exactly the semantics
    those assertions compare against. Tensor/tensor, tensor/scalar and
    scalar/scalar (Python ``int``/``float``) callsites are all covered.
    """
    return torch.floor_divide(a, b)
